// Every node's help, as data. GENERATED by help_lines/defgen.py -- edit
// help_lines/entries_*.json and run `python3 help_lines/gen.py`.
//
// THE SAME X-MACRO SHAPE AS words.def AND errors.def, and for the third time
// the same reason: a consumer defines SAT_HELP, includes this file, and undefines
// it again, so the table, the walk and the invariants all come from one list and
// cannot drift apart.
//
//     SAT_HELP(ident, head, prose, example)
//
// `ident` is the node's `words::NodeId`, and there is EXACTLY ONE ROW PER NODE
// IN words.def ORDER -- satellite_help/help_text.hpp asserts both at compile
// time, so a word appended to the registry with no entry here is a build error
// naming the node rather than a silence a person has to notice.
//
// `head` is the node this entry is asked about UNDER, which is what makes
// `satellite.help(satellite.console.input)` answer for `input()`, `input(prompt)`
// and `input(prompt, target)` together: all three carry head CONSOLE_INPUT_0.
// A node that heads its own group carries itself, and help_text.hpp asserts that
// a head is always its own head -- so the groups partition the registry and no
// entry can be unreachable.
//
// `prose` is what the entry says, `\n` for a line break and `\n\n` between
// paragraphs. `example` is the worked line, or "" where writing one would be
// inventing the language -- which is what an unbuilt path gets, and is the whole
// of DESIGN §1.1 applied to a help text.
//
// EVERY NON-EMPTY EXAMPLE IN HERE HAS BEEN RUN. help_lines/verify.py wraps each
// one into a program and executes it against the interpreter in this tree; the
// five statements that were confidently written and false are listed in
// help_lines/README.md, and they are why the script exists rather than why it is
// tidy.

#ifndef SAT_HELP
#define SAT_HELP(ident, head, prose, example)
#endif


// 1  satellite
SAT_HELP(SATELLITE, SATELLITE,
         "The root of the language, and the only word that is not under another\n"
         "one. Every path in satellite begins here, which is why its number is `1`\n"
         "and why every other number starts with a 1.\n"
         "\n"
         "On its own it names nothing you can call. Asking help about it is how you\n"
         "get the list of modules underneath, and that list is the language as it is\n"
         "built today rather than as it is planned."
         ,
         "satellite.help(satellite)"
         )

// 1 1  satellite.include
SAT_HELP(INCLUDE, INCLUDE,
         "Brings a body of words into the program. It is the first line of every\n"
         "satellite file, and until it has run the language's own names are not in\n"
         "scope, so a program without it cannot even reach `satellite.console`.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is a declaration and not a call. It sits at the top of the file,\n"
         "outside any capsule, and nothing is returned by it."
         ,
         "satellite.include(satellite)\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main()\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"the language is in scope\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 1 0  satellite.include()
SAT_HELP(INCLUDE_0, INCLUDE,
         "The bare shape, written with nothing between the parentheses. It parses,\n"
         "and it includes nothing, which makes it a way of saying the line is\n"
         "deliberate while the thing it will name is still being decided.\n"
         "\n"
         "It was the shape that refused until M17 taught the parser to accept an\n"
         "empty argument list here."
         ,
         "satellite.include()\n"
         "satellite.include(satellite)\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main()\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"the empty one included nothing\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 1 1  satellite.include(satellite)
SAT_HELP(INCLUDE_SATELLITE, INCLUDE,
         "Includes the language itself, and this is the line you actually type. It\n"
         "puts every built-in module in scope for the rest of the file:\n"
         "`satellite.console`, `satellite.container`, `satellite.variable` and the\n"
         "rest.\n"
         "\n"
         "The word `satellite` inside the parentheses is part of the path's number\n"
         "rather than an argument, so nothing is evaluated and you cannot pass a\n"
         "name there."
         ,
         "satellite.include(satellite)\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main()\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"Hello, World!\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 1 2  satellite.include(spaceship)
SAT_HELP(INCLUDE_SPACESHIP, INCLUDE,
         "Includes another satellite file, a spaceship, so its capsules can be\n"
         "called from this one.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built.** The path is numbered and the parser accepts it, but nothing\n"
         "loads a second file yet, so a program that writes this line refuses when\n"
         "it runs. M25 is the milestone that builds it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 2  satellite.capsule
SAT_HELP(CAPSULE, CAPSULE,
         "Declares a capsule, which is what this language calls a function. The\n"
         "name you give it is yours, the parameters are typed, and the body is a\n"
         "block in braces on the lines below.\n"
         "\n"
         "A capsule hands a value back with `satellite.return`. One that falls off\n"
         "the end of its body has simply finished, and needs no return at all."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule announce(satellite.variable.string what)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(what)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 2 0  satellite.capsule()
SAT_HELP(CAPSULE_0, CAPSULE,
         "Calling one. You write the capsule's own name and hand it arguments in\n"
         "the order it declared its parameters, so the path you type is never the\n"
         "word `capsule` itself.\n"
         "\n"
         "A capsule can call itself, and each call gets its own frame, so the\n"
         "parameters of the call in progress are never the ones the caller is\n"
         "holding."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule double_it(satellite.variable.number n)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.return(n * 2)\n"
         "}\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.console.display(double_it(21))"
         )

// 1 3  satellite.main
SAT_HELP(MAIN, MAIN,
         "Where the program starts. Every program declares exactly one, written as\n"
         "a capsule whose name is `satellite.main`, and running the file runs its\n"
         "body from the first line to the last.\n"
         "\n"
         "**You can also call it yourself**, from another capsule or from inside\n"
         "main, and it behaves like any other capsule when you do: it gets its own\n"
         "frame each time, and it counts its arguments. If it declares the argument\n"
         "list, a call that hands it nothing is refused rather than run."
         ,
         "satellite.include(satellite)\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.library.n = 0\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main()\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.library.n = satellite.library.n + 1\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(satellite.library.n)\n"
         "    satellite.statement.if (satellite.library.n < 3)\n"
         "    {\n"
         "        satellite.main()\n"
         "    }\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 3 0  satellite.main()
SAT_HELP(MAIN_0, MAIN,
         "The parameter list, and it takes either nothing or the command line.\n"
         "Written empty, the program ignores how it was started. Written with a\n"
         "list of strings, that name holds the arguments the program was given, and\n"
         "the name is yours to choose.\n"
         "\n"
         "Displaying it prints the arguments as a list, so a program started with\n"
         "none prints `[]`. It is also the shape a call to main takes, and calling\n"
         "one that declares a parameter with no argument is refused."
         ,
         "satellite.include(satellite)\n"
         "\n"
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4  satellite.container
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER, CONTAINER,
         "The things that hold other things. There are two: a list, which keeps\n"
         "values in order, and a map, which keeps values under keys.\n"
         "\n"
         "Both say what they hold inside angle brackets when you declare them, and\n"
         "both are made by calling the bare shape rather than by the declaration\n"
         "alone."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 0  satellite.container()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_0, CONTAINER,
         "The bare shape. There is no container called `container`; the word is the\n"
         "family and `list` or `map` is what you write."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 4 1  satellite.container.map
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP, CONTAINER_MAP,
         "Values kept under keys. The declaration names both types, the key's and\n"
         "the value's, separated by a comma inside the angle brackets.\n"
         "\n"
         "It remembers the order things were put in, so walking its keys gives them\n"
         "back in that order rather than in some order of its own."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m)"
         )

// 1 4 1 0  satellite.container.map()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_0, CONTAINER_MAP,
         "Makes an empty map. A declaration on its own holds nothing, so this call\n"
         "is what actually constructs one.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the same shape for every map whatever it holds; the types are in the\n"
         "declaration on the left."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 1 1  satellite.container.map.set(k, v)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_SET, CONTAINER_MAP_SET,
         "Puts a value under a key. A key that is not there yet is added; one that\n"
         "is already there has its value replaced.\n"
         "\n"
         "The square bracket form does the same thing and reads better when the key\n"
         "is a literal."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "m[\"washer\"] = 4\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m)"
         )

// 1 4 1 2  satellite.container.map.get(k)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_GET, CONTAINER_MAP_GET,
         "Takes the value out from under a key.\n"
         "\n"
         "Ask `has` first if you are not sure the key is there, since asking for a\n"
         "key that is missing is a refusal rather than a quiet empty answer."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.get(\"bolt\"))\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m[\"bolt\"])"
         )

// 1 4 1 3  satellite.container.map.has(k)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_HAS, CONTAINER_MAP_HAS,
         "Answers true when the key is in the map.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the test to write before `get`, and it is also how you tell an\n"
         "absent key from a key whose value happens to be zero."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.has(\"bolt\"))\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.has(\"rivet\"))"
         )

// 1 4 1 4  satellite.container.map.size
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_SIZE, CONTAINER_MAP_SIZE,
         "How many pairs the map holds."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "m.set(\"nut\", 9)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.size())"
         )

// 1 4 1 5  satellite.container.map.empty
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_EMPTY, CONTAINER_MAP_EMPTY,
         "Answers true when the map holds no pairs at all."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 1 6  satellite.container.map.clear
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_CLEAR, CONTAINER_MAP_CLEAR,
         "Throws away every pair, leaving the map empty and ready to use again."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "m.clear()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 1 7  satellite.container.map.remove(k)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_REMOVE, CONTAINER_MAP_REMOVE,
         "Takes one key and its value out of the map.\n"
         "\n"
         "The order of what is left is unchanged, so removing from the middle does\n"
         "not shuffle the rest."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "m.set(\"nut\", 9)\n"
         "m.remove(\"bolt\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m)"
         )

// 1 4 1 8  satellite.container.map.keys
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_KEYS, CONTAINER_MAP_KEYS,
         "Hands back every key as a list, in the order they were put in.\n"
         "\n"
         "Because that order is kept rather than arbitrary, a walk over the keys is\n"
         "something a program can rely on."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "m.set(\"nut\", 9)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.keys())"
         )

// 1 4 1 9  satellite.container.map.values
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_VALUES, CONTAINER_MAP_VALUES,
         "Hands back every value as a list, in the same order the keys come back\n"
         "in, so the two lists line up position by position."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"bolt\", 7)\n"
         "m.set(\"nut\", 9)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.values())"
         )

// 1 4 1 10  satellite.container.map.search(pattern)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_MAP_SEARCH, CONTAINER_MAP_SEARCH,
         "Finds pairs whose key is close to the pattern, rather than exactly equal\n"
         "to it. How close is close enough is a dial, `satellite.system.threshold`,\n"
         "running from an exact match up to a substring either way.\n"
         "\n"
         "What comes back is a list of maps, one per hit, each carrying the value,\n"
         "the key, the path and the score."
         ,
         "satellite.container.map<satellite.variable.string, satellite.variable.number> m = satellite.container.map()\n"
         "m.set(\"washer\", 4)\n"
         "satellite.system.threshold(6)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(m.search(\"was\"))"
         )

// 1 4 2  satellite.container.list
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST,
         "Values in order, counted from zero. The declaration names what it holds\n"
         "inside angle brackets, and everything in it is that one type.\n"
         "\n"
         "A position in square brackets reads or writes one value, a negative one\n"
         "counts back from the end, and two positions with a colon between them\n"
         "take a slice."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l[0])\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l[-1])"
         )

// 1 4 2 0  satellite.container.list()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_0, CONTAINER_LIST,
         "Makes an empty list. The declaration holds nothing on its own, so this\n"
         "call is what constructs one."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> parts = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(parts.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 2 1  satellite.container.list.append
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_APPEND, CONTAINER_LIST_APPEND,
         "Adds one value to the end. It changes the list you called it on rather\n"
         "than handing back a new one.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is how a list gets built, since a list constructed empty has nothing\n"
         "in it and there is no literal form."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.append(2)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 2  satellite.container.list.size
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SIZE, CONTAINER_LIST_SIZE,
         "How many values the list holds.\n"
         "\n"
         "It counts what is there now, so it is right after appends and removals\n"
         "without being told."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.size())"
         )

// 1 4 2 3  satellite.container.list.sort()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_0, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_0,
         "Puts the values in order, smallest first, changing the list in place.\n"
         "\n"
         "Strings sort as text and numbers sort as numbers, so a list sorts the way\n"
         "the type it holds compares."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.append(2)\n"
         "l.sort()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 4  satellite.container.list.sort(direction)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DIRECTION, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_0,
         "Sorts in the direction you name, `\"up\"` or `\"down\"`.\n"
         "\n"
         "The two spellings fold onto the plain sort and the sort_down beside it, so\n"
         "this is the same work reached by a name rather than a different one."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.sort(\"down\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)\n"
         "l.sort(\"up\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 5  satellite.container.list.sort_down()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DOWN_0, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DOWN_0,
         "Puts the values in order largest first, changing the list in place.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the plain sort turned round, and `sort(\"down\")` is the same thing\n"
         "said the other way."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.sort_down()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 6  satellite.container.list.sort_down(key)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DOWN_KEY, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DOWN_0,
         "Sorts largest first by a key you name, for a list holding maps.\n"
         "\n"
         "The key is the name to look under in each map, so a list of records\n"
         "sorts by one of their fields."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 4 2 7  satellite.container.list.sort_up(key)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_UP, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_UP,
         "Sorts smallest first by a key you name, for a list holding maps.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the pair to sort_down with a key, and the key is the name to look\n"
         "under in each map."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 4 2 8  satellite.container.list.contains(x)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_CONTAINS, CONTAINER_LIST_CONTAINS,
         "Answers true when the value is somewhere in the list.\n"
         "\n"
         "When you need to know where rather than whether, `index_of` says."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> parts = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "parts.append(\"bolt\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(parts.contains(\"bolt\"))"
         )

// 1 4 2 9  satellite.container.list.index_of(x)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_INDEX_OF, CONTAINER_LIST_INDEX_OF,
         "Answers the position of the first place the value appears, counting from\n"
         "zero."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> parts = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "parts.append(\"bolt\")\n"
         "parts.append(\"nut\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(parts.index_of(\"nut\"))"
         )

// 1 4 2 10  satellite.container.list.empty
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_EMPTY, CONTAINER_LIST_EMPTY,
         "Answers true when the list holds nothing.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the honest test, since an empty list is not itself false."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 2 11  satellite.container.list.clear
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_CLEAR, CONTAINER_LIST_CLEAR,
         "Throws away every value, leaving the list empty and ready to use again."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.clear()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 2 12  satellite.container.list.first
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_FIRST, CONTAINER_LIST_FIRST,
         "The value at the front, without taking it out.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the same as position zero and reads better when the point is that it\n"
         "is the first one."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.first())"
         )

// 1 4 2 13  satellite.container.list.last
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_LAST, CONTAINER_LIST_LAST,
         "The value at the end, without taking it out.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the same as position -1 and says what it means more plainly."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.last())"
         )

// 1 4 2 14  satellite.container.list.truncate(n)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_TRUNCATE, CONTAINER_LIST_TRUNCATE,
         "Cuts the list down to the length you give, throwing away everything past\n"
         "it. A length longer than the list changes nothing."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.append(2)\n"
         "l.truncate(2)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 15  satellite.container.list.reserve(n)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_RESERVE, CONTAINER_LIST_RESERVE,
         "Says roughly how many values are coming, so the list can make room once\n"
         "instead of growing as it goes.\n"
         "\n"
         "It changes nothing you can observe except speed. The list is still empty\n"
         "afterwards."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.reserve(1000)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.empty())"
         )

// 1 4 2 16  satellite.container.list.remove_first()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_FIRST_0, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_FIRST_0,
         "Takes the front value out and shuffles the rest down."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.remove_first()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 17  satellite.container.list.remove_last()
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_LAST_0, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_LAST_0,
         "Takes the end value out, which costs nothing since nothing has to move."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.remove_last()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 18  satellite.container.list.remove_at(n)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_AT, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_AT,
         "Takes out the value at the position you name and closes the gap."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.append(2)\n"
         "l.remove_at(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 19  satellite.container.list.remove(x)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE,
         "Takes out the value you name rather than the position, removing the first\n"
         "place it appears.\n"
         "\n"
         "A quoted word is a value here like any other. It did not used to be: until\n"
         "M19 the compiler read `\"bolt\"` as the name of an option, because `remove_first`,\n"
         "`remove_last` and `remove_at` sit beside `remove` and it guessed from the\n"
         "spelling that those were three options rather than three separate words. They\n"
         "are separate words, and which words take options is written down now instead\n"
         "of guessed at."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> parts = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "parts.append(\"bolt\")\n"
         "parts.append(\"nut\")\n"
         "parts.remove(\"bolt\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(parts)"
         )

// 1 4 2 20  satellite.container.list.insert(n, x)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_INSERT, CONTAINER_LIST_INSERT,
         "Puts a value in at the position you name, pushing everything from there\n"
         "on one place along."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(2)\n"
         "l.insert(1, 9)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 21  satellite.container.list.join(separator)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_JOIN, CONTAINER_LIST_JOIN,
         "Runs the values together into one string with the separator between\n"
         "them, and nothing after the last one.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the other half of a string's `split`."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> parts = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "parts.append(\"a\")\n"
         "parts.append(\"b\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(parts.join(\", \"))"
         )

// 1 4 2 22  satellite.container.list.reverse
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_REVERSE, CONTAINER_LIST_REVERSE,
         "Turns the list back to front in place."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.reverse()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l)"
         )

// 1 4 2 23  satellite.container.list.sum
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SUM, CONTAINER_LIST_SUM,
         "Adds every value together. The list has to hold numbers.\n"
         "\n"
         "Since a number here has no width, a long list of large values sums\n"
         "exactly rather than wrapping."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "l.append(2)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.sum())"
         )

// 1 4 2 24  satellite.container.list.max
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_MAX, CONTAINER_LIST_MAX,
         "The largest value in the list."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.max())"
         )

// 1 4 2 25  satellite.container.list.min
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_MIN, CONTAINER_LIST_MIN,
         "The smallest value in the list."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.number> l = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "l.append(3)\n"
         "l.append(1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(l.min())"
         )

// 1 4 2 26  satellite.container.list.search(pattern)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_LIST_SEARCH, CONTAINER_LIST_SEARCH,
         "Finds values close to the pattern rather than exactly equal to it, and\n"
         "hands back a list of maps, one per hit, carrying the value, the key, the\n"
         "path and the score.\n"
         "\n"
         "The shorter form, a pattern in square brackets, gives just the values\n"
         "that matched. How loose a match may be is `satellite.system.threshold`."
         ,
         "satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> parts = satellite.container.list()\n"
         "parts.append(\"washer\")\n"
         "satellite.system.threshold(6)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(parts.search(\"was\"))"
         )

// 1 4 3  satellite.container.arguments
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS,
         "The type of the object `satellite.main` is handed — the command line and\n"
         "the machine's own answers, in one value.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is two containers at once, and its ten methods say which half they are\n"
         "asking about. `length()`, `first()`, `last()` and `contains(x)` are the\n"
         "command line; `count()`, `keys()`, `has(k)` and `get(k)` are every entry,\n"
         "the machine's facts included; `to_string()` and `lines()` are the whole of\n"
         "it as text.\n"
         "\n"
         "**The name is yours.** A program never writes this path — it writes the\n"
         "name it gave the parameter, and the methods are reached through that."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.length())\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 1  satellite.container.arguments.length
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LENGTH, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LENGTH,
         "How many words were on the command line, the program's own name included.\n"
         "\n"
         "**The command line and nothing else.** The machine's facts are reached by\n"
         "name, never by number, so `for (i = 1; i < arguments.length(); i = i + 1)`\n"
         "walks exactly what somebody typed.\n"
         "\n"
         "`satellite.library.main.arguments.length` `1 14 1 1 9` is the same question\n"
         "and the same number, asked off the object rather than through its type."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.length())\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 2  satellite.container.arguments.count
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_COUNT, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_COUNT,
         "How many entries the object holds altogether — the command line and every\n"
         "fact about the machine.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is a different number from `length()` on purpose, and the two words were\n"
         "split so that neither could answer the other's question. A program that\n"
         "means \"all of them\" says `count`; one that means \"what was typed\" says\n"
         "`length`."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.count())\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 3  satellite.container.arguments.keys
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_KEYS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_KEYS,
         "Every entry's name, as a list of strings, in the order they are printed.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is how a program walks what it was given rather than knowing the list in\n"
         "advance — the command line's `program` and `argument_1`, then\n"
         "`machine.threads` and the rest."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> names = arguments.keys()\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(names.size())\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 4  satellite.container.arguments.to_string
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_TO_STRING, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_TO_STRING,
         "The whole object as the text `display` prints: one entry per line, names\n"
         "beside the data.\n"
         "\n"
         "Not the command line alone. Showing only that would hide the facts that are\n"
         "the point of the object."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.variable.string all = arguments.to_string()\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(all)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 5  satellite.container.arguments.lines
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LINES, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LINES,
         "The same text as `to_string()`.\n"
         "\n"
         "Two words for one answer, kept because both readings are natural: one asks\n"
         "what the object looks like written down, the other asks for its lines."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.variable.string all = arguments.lines()\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(all)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 6  satellite.container.arguments.has(k)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_HAS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_HAS,
         "Whether an entry of that name exists, as a yes or no.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the question `get(k)` refuses, so a program can ask before it asks."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.has(\"machine.threads\"))\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 7  satellite.container.arguments.get(k)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_GET, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_GET,
         "The entry of that name.\n"
         "\n"
         "A name nothing answers to is refused rather than answered with nothing —\n"
         "`has(k)` is how to ask first. The names are the ones `keys()` lists."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.get(\"machine.threads\"))\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 8  satellite.container.arguments.first
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_FIRST, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_FIRST,
         "The first word of the command line, which is the program itself."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.first())\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 9  satellite.container.arguments.last
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LAST, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LAST,
         "The last word of the command line.\n"
         "\n"
         "With no arguments given it is the program's own name, because that is the\n"
         "only word there is."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.last())\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 3 10  satellite.container.arguments.contains(x)
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_CONTAINS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_CONTAINS,
         "Whether that word was on the command line.\n"
         "\n"
         "The command line only — it does not look through the machine's facts, which\n"
         "`keys()` and `get(k)` are for."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.contains(\"--quiet\"))\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 4 4  satellite.container.result
SAT_HELP(CONTAINER_RESULT, CONTAINER_RESULT,
         "A result carrying either an answer or a reason it failed.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built.** The path is numbered and no milestone has reached it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 5  satellite.console
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE, CONSOLE,
         "The terminal the program is running in, both halves of it. Everything the\n"
         "program writes out goes through here, and everything the person types in\n"
         "comes back through here.\n"
         "\n"
         "Writing is printed by a thread of its own, so a program that prints in a\n"
         "tight loop is not waiting on the terminal to keep up."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(\"Hello, World!\")"
         )

// 1 5 0  satellite.console()
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_0, CONSOLE,
         "The bare shape. The console is a module rather than a thing you construct,\n"
         "so there is nothing to call here and no value comes back.\n"
         "\n"
         "What you want is one of the words underneath, and asking help about the\n"
         "module lists them."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 5 1  satellite.console.display
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_DISPLAY, CONSOLE_DISPLAY,
         "Prints one value and moves to the next line. It takes any type, and it\n"
         "prints the value the way that type renders itself, so a list prints its\n"
         "brackets and a map prints its pairs without you formatting anything.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the most-called word in the language and the first one most people\n"
         "type."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting.size())"
         )

// 1 5 2  satellite.console.input()
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_INPUT_0, CONSOLE_INPUT_0,
         "Reads one line the person types and hands it back as a string, without\n"
         "printing anything first. The newline they press is not part of what you\n"
         "get.\n"
         "\n"
         "It waits. If nothing is there to type, the program stops on this line\n"
         "until something is."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string answer = satellite.console.input()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(answer)"
         )

// 1 5 3  satellite.console.input(prompt)
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_INPUT_PROMPT, CONSOLE_INPUT_0,
         "Prints the prompt you give it, then reads one line back. This is the\n"
         "ordinary shape, because a question and the wait for its answer belong on\n"
         "the same line.\n"
         "\n"
         "The prompt is printed as you wrote it, so put your own space or colon at\n"
         "the end if you want one."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string name = satellite.console.input(\"what is your name? \")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(name)"
         )

// 1 5 4  satellite.console.input(prompt, target)
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_INPUT_PROMPT_TARGET, CONSOLE_INPUT_0,
         "Prints the prompt and reads the answer straight into a variable you\n"
         "name, instead of handing it back to be assigned.\n"
         "\n"
         "The second argument is the variable itself and not its value, so the\n"
         "variable has to be declared before the line runs. It is the one place in\n"
         "the language where an argument is a name rather than a thing."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string name = \"\"\n"
         "satellite.console.input(\"what is your name? \", name)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(name)"
         )

// 1 5 5  satellite.console.typed()
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_TYPED_0, CONSOLE_TYPED_0,
         "Reads a line **if one is already waiting**, and answers `nothing` if it is\n"
         "not. It never waits, which is the whole point of it: a program with work to\n"
         "do can check on the person between pieces of that work.\n"
         "\n"
         "What comes back is the line itself and not a yes-or-no, so hold it in a\n"
         "`satellite.variable.variant` and ask whether it is holding nothing."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant line = satellite.console.typed()\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (line.holds(\"nothing\"))\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"nobody has typed yet\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 5 6  satellite.console.width
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_WIDTH, CONSOLE_WIDTH,
         "How many columns wide the terminal is right now. It is read when you ask\n"
         "rather than remembered, so a window the person resizes gives a new answer\n"
         "on the next line.\n"
         "\n"
         "Use it to decide how much fits before you print it."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.console.width)"
         )

// 1 5 7  satellite.console.height
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_HEIGHT, CONSOLE_HEIGHT,
         "How many rows tall the terminal is right now, read fresh the same way the\n"
         "width is.\n"
         "\n"
         "Together the two are what you need to draw anything that should fill the\n"
         "screen rather than scroll off it."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.console.height)"
         )

// 1 5 8  satellite.console.clear()
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_CLEAR_0, CONSOLE_CLEAR_0,
         "Wipes the screen and leaves the cursor at the top left. Nothing the\n"
         "program has printed is recoverable afterwards.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the first line of anything that redraws rather than scrolls."
         ,
         "satellite.console.clear()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"a fresh screen\")"
         )

// 1 5 9  satellite.console.home()
SAT_HELP(CONSOLE_HOME_0, CONSOLE_HOME_0,
         "Moves the cursor back to the top left without erasing anything. What was\n"
         "on the screen stays there until something is printed over it.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is what you want for a display that updates in place, because\n"
         "clearing first is what makes a redraw flicker."
         ,
         "satellite.console.home()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"printed over the top line\")"
         )

// 1 6  satellite.variable
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE, VARIABLE,
         "Every kind of value a name can hold. A declaration names the kind first\n"
         "and the name second, and the kind never changes afterwards.\n"
         "\n"
         "A declaration on its own holds nothing. It is the assignment beside it, or\n"
         "a later one, that puts a value in."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.variable.number count = 3\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(count)"
         )

// 1 6 0  satellite.variable()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_0, VARIABLE,
         "The bare shape. There is no type called `variable`; the word is the family\n"
         "and one of the kinds underneath is what you write.\n"
         "\n"
         "Asking help about the family lists the kinds, and the ones that are built\n"
         "are the ones you can declare today."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 1  satellite.variable.string
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING,
         "Text. It is written in double quotes and it can be any length; there is\n"
         "no limit here and no separate character type.\n"
         "\n"
         "It carries sixteen methods, which are the words underneath. Every one is\n"
         "called on a declared name rather than on a literal, so give the text a\n"
         "name first."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "greeting.append(\", World!\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting)"
         )

// 1 6 1 0  satellite.variable.string()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_0, VARIABLE_STRING,
         "The bare shape, which is how you make an empty string when you have\n"
         "nothing to put in it yet.\n"
         "\n"
         "A declaration with no assignment holds nothing at all, which is not the\n"
         "same as holding an empty string, so write the quotes if empty is what you\n"
         "mean."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string answer = \"\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(answer.empty())"
         )

// 1 6 1 1  satellite.variable.string.size
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_SIZE, VARIABLE_STRING_SIZE,
         "How many characters the string holds. An empty one answers zero.\n"
         "\n"
         "It counts what is there rather than remembering what was put in, so it is\n"
         "correct after an append or a trim without you doing anything."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting.size())"
         )

// 1 6 1 2  satellite.variable.string.empty
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_EMPTY, VARIABLE_STRING_EMPTY,
         "Answers true when the string holds no characters at all.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the honest test, because this language has no truthiness and an\n"
         "empty string is not itself false."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string answer = \"\"\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (answer.empty())\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"nothing was typed\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 6 1 3  satellite.variable.string.find(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_FIND, VARIABLE_STRING_FIND,
         "Answers where a piece of text first appears, counting from zero.\n"
         "\n"
         "Use it when you need the position. When you only need to know whether the\n"
         "text is in there at all, `contains` says so more plainly."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string line = \"bolt,nut,washer\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(line.find(\"nut\"))"
         )

// 1 6 1 4  satellite.variable.string.contains(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_CONTAINS, VARIABLE_STRING_CONTAINS,
         "Answers true when the piece of text appears anywhere in the string.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the question you usually mean, and it reads better in a condition\n"
         "than comparing a position against a number."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string line = \"bolt,nut,washer\"\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (line.contains(\"nut\"))\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"found it\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 6 1 5  satellite.variable.string.substring(start, end)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_SUBSTRING, VARIABLE_STRING_SUBSTRING,
         "Hands back the piece between two positions, counting from zero. The first\n"
         "position is included and the second is not, so the difference between them\n"
         "is the length you get.\n"
         "\n"
         "The string you called it on is unchanged."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello, World!\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting.substring(0, 5))"
         )

// 1 6 1 6  satellite.variable.string.starts_with(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_STARTS_WITH, VARIABLE_STRING_STARTS_WITH,
         "Answers true when the string begins with the text you give it.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the cheap way to sort lines into kinds without picking them apart\n"
         "first."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string line = \"error: something went wrong\"\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (line.starts_with(\"error\"))\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"that is a complaint\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 6 1 7  satellite.variable.string.ends_with(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_ENDS_WITH, VARIABLE_STRING_ENDS_WITH,
         "Answers true when the string finishes with the text you give it.\n"
         "\n"
         "The usual use is a file name's ending, since the language has no separate\n"
         "idea of an extension."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string name = \"program.satl\"\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (name.ends_with(\".satl\"))\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"that is satellite source\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 6 1 8  satellite.variable.string.lower
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_LOWER, VARIABLE_STRING_LOWER,
         "Hands back the same text with every letter in lower case, and leaves the\n"
         "original alone.\n"
         "\n"
         "Use it on both sides when you want a comparison that ignores case, since\n"
         "comparing strings compares them exactly."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string shout = \"HELLO\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(shout.lower())"
         )

// 1 6 1 9  satellite.variable.string.upper
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_UPPER, VARIABLE_STRING_UPPER,
         "Hands back the same text with every letter in upper case, and leaves the\n"
         "original alone.\n"
         "\n"
         "It answers a new string, so assign it or print it; calling it and ignoring\n"
         "the answer changes nothing."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting.upper())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting)"
         )

// 1 6 1 10  satellite.variable.string.split(separator)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_SPLIT, VARIABLE_STRING_SPLIT,
         "Cuts the string wherever the separator appears and hands back the pieces\n"
         "as a list of strings.\n"
         "\n"
         "The separator itself is not in any of the pieces. This is how a line of\n"
         "comma-separated text becomes something you can walk."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string line = \"a,b,c\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(line.split(\",\"))"
         )

// 1 6 1 11  satellite.variable.string.trim
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_TRIM, VARIABLE_STRING_TRIM,
         "Hands back the text with the blank space taken off both ends, and leaves\n"
         "what is in the middle alone.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the first thing to do to a line somebody typed, because the space\n"
         "before their answer is not part of it."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string typed = \"   Ada   \"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(typed.trim())"
         )

// 1 6 1 12  satellite.variable.string.replace(a, b)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_REPLACE, VARIABLE_STRING_REPLACE,
         "Hands back the text with every appearance of the first piece swapped for\n"
         "the second.\n"
         "\n"
         "It replaces all of them and not just the first, and the string you called\n"
         "it on is unchanged."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string line = \"a,b,c\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(line.replace(\",\", \" and \"))"
         )

// 1 6 1 13  satellite.variable.string.to_number
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_TO_NUMBER, VARIABLE_STRING_TO_NUMBER,
         "Reads the text as a number and hands the number back. It is the other\n"
         "half of a number's `to_string`.\n"
         "\n"
         "The text has to actually be a number. This is what you call on a line the\n"
         "person typed before you can do arithmetic with it."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string typed = \"42\"\n"
         "satellite.variable.number n = typed.to_number()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(n * 2)"
         )

// 1 6 1 14  satellite.variable.string.append(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_APPEND, VARIABLE_STRING_APPEND,
         "Adds text to the end, and this one changes the string you called it on\n"
         "rather than handing back a new one.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the odd one out among the string methods for exactly that reason, so\n"
         "you do not assign its answer to anything."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "greeting.append(\", World!\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting)"
         )

// 1 6 1 15  satellite.variable.string.clear
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_CLEAR, VARIABLE_STRING_CLEAR,
         "Empties the string, leaving it holding no characters. Like append, it\n"
         "changes the string you called it on.\n"
         "\n"
         "Afterwards `empty()` answers true and `size()` answers zero."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "greeting.clear()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting.empty())"
         )

// 1 6 1 16  satellite.variable.string.at(n)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_AT, VARIABLE_STRING_AT,
         "Hands back the one character at the position you name, counting from\n"
         "zero.\n"
         "\n"
         "There is no character type here, so what comes back is a string holding\n"
         "one character."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string greeting = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(greeting.at(0))"
         )

// 1 6 1 17  satellite.variable.string.resolved
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_STRING_RESOLVED, VARIABLE_STRING_RESOLVED,
         "The string with its live values answered by the machine.\n"
         "\n"
         "Six escapes are read when they are USED rather than when the file is lexed\n"
         "— `\\home`, `\\user`, `\\threads`, `\\memtotal`, `\\memused` and `\\cwd`. Each is\n"
         "stored as one character, and `display` asks the machine for it on the way\n"
         "to the screen; this is that same question, asked where a program can keep\n"
         "the answer.\n"
         "\n"
         "Every other method sees what is stored, which is why `size` counts\n"
         "`\"\\threads\"` as one. A string with no live value in it answers itself."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.string live = \"this machine runs \\threads threads\"\n"
         "satellite.variable.string said = live.resolved()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(said)"
         )

// 1 6 2  satellite.variable.file
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE, VARIABLE_FILE,
         "A file on disk, held as a value you can read from and write to. It is a\n"
         "**reference type**: two names for one open file are two names for one open\n"
         "file, so closing it through either one closes it for both. Every other value\n"
         "in the language is copied when you assign it; this one is shared.\n"
         "\n"
         "You never declare one and fill it in. `satellite.file.open` and\n"
         "`satellite.file.new` are what make one, and both hand you a handle that is\n"
         "already open.\n"
         "\n"
         "**A file that would not open is a value and not an error.** The handle comes\n"
         "back anyway, `ok` answers false, and `error` says why -- so asking whether a\n"
         "file is there never stops the program that asked."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.open(\"no_such_file\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.ok())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.error())"
         )

// 1 6 2 0  satellite.variable.file()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_0, VARIABLE_FILE,
         "The bare shape. A file is written as a type, never called, so this number is\n"
         "reserved and nothing reaches it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 2 1  satellite.variable.file.new
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_NEW, VARIABLE_FILE_NEW,
         "**Numbered, and deliberately empty.** There is one way to make a file and it\n"
         "is `satellite.file.new(path)`, the module word. This number exists because the\n"
         "language's design once sketched `my_file.new()` as an example of a call that\n"
         "would be confusing, and the example got a number before anyone decided it was\n"
         "not a word.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is left unbuilt rather than quietly made an alias, because two spellings\n"
         "for one thing is worse than one spelling and a note saying so."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 2 2  satellite.variable.file.open
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_OPEN, VARIABLE_FILE_OPEN,
         "Opens a handle again -- one that was closed, or one whose first open failed\n"
         "and whose reason has since gone away. It answers true or false.\n"
         "\n"
         "**It is not the same word as `satellite.file.open`,** which takes a path and\n"
         "makes a handle. This one takes no arguments: it already knows which file it is\n"
         "about, and it reopens it in the mode it was opened with the first time.\n"
         "\n"
         "A handle that is already open answers true and nothing happens. A reopened\n"
         "handle reads from the beginning again."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"reopen_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"one\")\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.open())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.read_line())\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"reopen_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 6 2 3  satellite.variable.file.read_line
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_READ_LINE, VARIABLE_FILE_READ_LINE,
         "Reads the next line, **or nothing** when there are no more. The newline\n"
         "itself is not part of what you get.\n"
         "\n"
         "Each call moves forward, so calling it in a loop walks the file. An empty\n"
         "line and nothing are two different answers: an empty line is a line that was\n"
         "there and had no characters in it, and nothing means the file ended.\n"
         "\n"
         "Declare the variable as a `satellite.variable.variant` if you mean to handle\n"
         "the ending yourself and ask it `holding`. Declare it as a string and the\n"
         "program stops by name at the line that first uses it after the end, which is\n"
         "often exactly what you want."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"lines_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"first\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"second\")\n"
         "satellite.variable.variant line = f.read_line()\n"
         "satellite.statement.while (line.holding() != \"nothing\")\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(line.held())\n"
         "    line = f.read_line()\n"
         "}\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"lines_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 6 2 4  satellite.variable.file.write_line(s)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_WRITE_LINE, VARIABLE_FILE_WRITE_LINE,
         "Writes the text you give it **and a newline after it**. It answers true, or\n"
         "false if the write itself failed -- a full disk, for instance.\n"
         "\n"
         "Use `write` instead when you want exactly the bytes and no newline. The two\n"
         "verbs exist so that neither case has to be worked around: a file whose last\n"
         "line has no newline, and a line put together from several calls, are both\n"
         "writable."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"write_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"a line\")\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.variable.file back = satellite.file.open(\"write_demo.txt\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(back.read_all())\n"
         "back.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"write_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 6 2 5  satellite.variable.file.read_all
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_READ_ALL, VARIABLE_FILE_READ_ALL,
         "The whole file, from the beginning, as one string.\n"
         "\n"
         "It shares its place in the file with `read_line`, and it leaves that place at\n"
         "the end -- so a `read_line` straight after a `read_all` answers nothing. That\n"
         "is the honest report of a file that has just been read from end to end."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"all_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"one\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"two\")\n"
         "satellite.variable.string whole = f.read_all()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(whole.size())\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"all_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 6 2 6  satellite.variable.file.close
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_CLOSE, VARIABLE_FILE_CLOSE,
         "Closes the file and answers whether that worked.\n"
         "\n"
         "**It answers rather than just happening,** because closing is where writes\n"
         "actually reach the disk and where a full disk or a failing drive is reported.\n"
         "A program that has written something it cares about should look at what this\n"
         "says.\n"
         "\n"
         "Closing a file that is already closed is true and does nothing."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"close_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"safe\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.close())\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"close_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 6 2 7  satellite.variable.file.exists
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_EXISTS, VARIABLE_FILE_EXISTS,
         "Whether **this handle's own path** is still there.\n"
         "\n"
         "It asks about the name and not about the handle, so a closed handle still\n"
         "answers, and a file that was deleted while you held it open answers false.\n"
         "\n"
         "To ask about a path you have no handle for, use `satellite.file.exists`\n"
         "instead."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"exists_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.exists())\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(f)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.exists())"
         )

// 1 6 2 8  satellite.variable.file.ok
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_OK, VARIABLE_FILE_OK,
         "Whether the file is open.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the question the whole design of files is built around. Opening a\n"
         "file that is not there does not stop your program -- you get a handle that is\n"
         "not ok, and you decide what to do about it. `error` is the sentence that says\n"
         "why."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file good = satellite.file.new(\"ok_demo.txt\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(good.ok())\n"
         "good.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"ok_demo.txt\")\n"
         "satellite.variable.file bad = satellite.file.open(\"ok_demo.txt\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bad.ok())"
         )

// 1 6 2 9  satellite.variable.file.path
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_PATH, VARIABLE_FILE_PATH,
         "The path this handle was opened on, as a string.\n"
         "\n"
         "It answers whether or not the file is open, and whether or not the open\n"
         "worked -- a handle always knows which file it is about."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.open(\"nowhere/at/all\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.path())"
         )

// 1 6 2 10  satellite.variable.file.error
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_ERROR, VARIABLE_FILE_ERROR,
         "Why the last thing that went wrong went wrong, in plain words, or an empty\n"
         "string when nothing has.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the machine's own sentence -- \"No such file or directory\",\n"
         "\"Permission denied\" -- so it says something a person can act on rather than a\n"
         "number they have to look up.\n"
         "\n"
         "A successful reopen clears it, because a reason that no longer applies is\n"
         "worse than no reason."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.open(\"no_such_file\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.error())"
         )

// 1 6 2 11  satellite.variable.file.write(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FILE_WRITE, VARIABLE_FILE_WRITE,
         "Writes **exactly** what you give it -- no newline, nothing added. It answers\n"
         "true, or false if the write failed.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the verb for building a line out of pieces, for writing a file that\n"
         "should not end in a newline, and for writing bytes rather than text.\n"
         "`write_line` is the one that adds the newline for you."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"exact_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write(\"one\")\n"
         "f.write(\"two\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.read_all())\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"exact_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 6 3  satellite.variable.time
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_TIME, VARIABLE_TIME,
         "A moment, held as a value.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built as a type.** `satellite.time.now()` answers the clock today,\n"
         "but there is no declared time variable to keep one in yet. M29 is the\n"
         "calendar milestone that decides what a time value can be."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 3 0  satellite.variable.time()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_TIME_0, VARIABLE_TIME,
         "The bare shape. **Not built** — M29."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 4  satellite.variable.number
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER, VARIABLE_NUMBER,
         "A whole number, of any size. It does not overflow and it has no width, so\n"
         "a factorial that runs past what a machine word holds keeps being right.\n"
         "\n"
         "The sign is written out where it matters. Fifteen methods hang underneath\n"
         "it, and each is called on a declared name."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number count = 3\n"
         "satellite.console.display(count * 1000000000000)"
         )

// 1 6 4 0  satellite.variable.number()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_0, VARIABLE_NUMBER,
         "The bare shape, for a number you have nowhere to get a value from yet.\n"
         "\n"
         "A declaration holds nothing until something is assigned, so write `= 0` if\n"
         "zero is what you actually mean."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number total = 0\n"
         "satellite.console.display(total)"
         )

// 1 6 4 1  satellite.variable.number.shift_left(n)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_SHIFT_LEFT, VARIABLE_NUMBER_SHIFT_LEFT,
         "Moves the number's bits up by the count you give, which doubles it once\n"
         "per place.\n"
         "\n"
         "Because a number here has no width, nothing falls off the top; the value\n"
         "just gets larger."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number n = 1\n"
         "satellite.console.display(n.shift_left(10))"
         )

// 1 6 4 2  satellite.variable.number.max(a, b)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_MAX, VARIABLE_NUMBER_MAX,
         "Answers whichever is larger, the number you called it on or the one you\n"
         "hand it.\n"
         "\n"
         "Neither number is changed; what comes back is a new value."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number a = 3\n"
         "satellite.console.display(a.max(9))"
         )

// 1 6 4 3  satellite.variable.number.min(a, b)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_MIN, VARIABLE_NUMBER_MIN,
         "Answers whichever is smaller, the number you called it on or the one you\n"
         "hand it.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the pair to `max`, and the usual use of the two together is holding\n"
         "a value inside a range."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number a = 3\n"
         "satellite.console.display(a.min(9))"
         )

// 1 6 4 4  satellite.variable.number.abs(a)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_ABS, VARIABLE_NUMBER_ABS,
         "Answers the number without its sign, so a negative one comes back\n"
         "positive and a positive one comes back unchanged.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is what you want before comparing two distances, where the direction\n"
         "does not matter."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number drift = 0 - 7\n"
         "satellite.console.display(drift.abs())"
         )

// 1 6 4 5  satellite.variable.number.clamp(a, low, high)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_CLAMP, VARIABLE_NUMBER_CLAMP,
         "Holds the number inside a range. Give it the low end and the high end,\n"
         "and anything below or above comes back as the end it passed.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is `max` and `min` in one call, which is how it is usually wanted."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number n = 42\n"
         "satellite.console.display(n.clamp(0, 10))"
         )

// 1 6 4 6  satellite.variable.number.to_string
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_TO_STRING, VARIABLE_NUMBER_TO_STRING,
         "Renders the number as text. It is the other half of a string's\n"
         "`to_number`.\n"
         "\n"
         "Use it when you want to join a number onto a string, since the two types\n"
         "do not add to each other."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number count = 3\n"
         "satellite.variable.string line = \"there are \"\n"
         "line.append(count.to_string())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(line)"
         )

// 1 6 4 7  satellite.variable.number.floor
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_FLOOR, VARIABLE_NUMBER_FLOOR,
         "Rounds down, towards the smaller number, and answers a whole number.\n"
         "\n"
         "On a value that is already whole it changes nothing, so it is safe to\n"
         "call without checking first."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number f = 3.7\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.floor())"
         )

// 1 6 4 8  satellite.variable.number.ceil
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_CEIL, VARIABLE_NUMBER_CEIL,
         "Rounds up, towards the larger number, and answers a whole number.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the pair to `floor`, and the one you want when you are asking how\n"
         "many whole things are needed to cover an amount."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number f = 3.2\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.ceil())"
         )

// 1 6 4 9  satellite.variable.number.round
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_ROUND, VARIABLE_NUMBER_ROUND,
         "Rounds to the nearest whole number. A value exactly halfway goes away\n"
         "from zero, so 2.5 becomes 3 and -2.5 becomes -3.\n"
         "\n"
         "That rule is written down rather than inherited, because halfway is the\n"
         "only case where languages disagree."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number f = 2.5\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.round())"
         )

// 1 6 4 10  satellite.variable.number.power(a, b)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_POWER, VARIABLE_NUMBER_POWER,
         "Raises the number to the power you give it.\n"
         "\n"
         "The answer is a float, so hold it in one or pass it straight to display. A\n"
         "number here has no width, so a large power gets a large answer rather than\n"
         "a wrong one."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number two = 2\n"
         "satellite.console.display(two.power(10))"
         )

// 1 6 4 11  satellite.variable.number.shift_right(n)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_SHIFT_RIGHT, VARIABLE_NUMBER_SHIFT_RIGHT,
         "Moves the number's bits down by the count you give, which halves it once\n"
         "per place and throws away what falls off the bottom.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the pair to `shift_left`."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number n = 1024\n"
         "satellite.console.display(n.shift_right(10))"
         )

// 1 6 4 12  satellite.variable.number.modulus(a, b)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_MODULUS, VARIABLE_NUMBER_MODULUS,
         "Answers what is left over after dividing by the number you hand it.\n"
         "\n"
         "The usual use is asking whether something divides evenly, which is a\n"
         "remainder of zero."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number n = 17\n"
         "satellite.console.display(n.modulus(5))"
         )

// 1 6 4 13  satellite.variable.number.truncate(a)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_TRUNCATE, VARIABLE_NUMBER_TRUNCATE,
         "Throws away everything after the decimal point and answers the whole part,\n"
         "without rounding either way.\n"
         "\n"
         "It differs from `floor` on negative values: truncating -3.7 gives -3, and\n"
         "flooring it gives -4."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number down = 0 - 2.7\n"
         "satellite.console.display(down.truncate())"
         )

// 1 6 4 14  satellite.variable.number.sqrt(a)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_SQRT, VARIABLE_NUMBER_SQRT,
         "Answers the square root.\n"
         "\n"
         "The answer is a float, because most square roots are not whole numbers,\n"
         "so hold it in one."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number n = 144\n"
         "satellite.console.display(n.sqrt())"
         )

// 1 6 4 15  satellite.variable.number.digits
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NUMBER_DIGITS, VARIABLE_NUMBER_DIGITS,
         "How many digits the number is written with.\n"
         "\n"
         "Since a number here has no width, this is a real question with a real\n"
         "answer however large the value gets. It wants a whole number, so a float\n"
         "is refused rather than guessed at."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number big = 123456789\n"
         "satellite.console.display(big.digits())"
         )

// 1 6 5  satellite.variable.binary
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY, VARIABLE_BINARY,
         "A run of bits, written `b1010`. **The width is part of the value**, so\n"
         "`b0010` and `b10` are not the same thing -- which is why this is a type of its\n"
         "own and not a number written in another base.\n"
         "\n"
         "It displays exactly as it was written, leading zeros and all, and `b0010 == b10`\n"
         "is false for the same reason. Ask `width()` for how many bits there are,\n"
         "`to_number()` for what they are worth, and `as_number()` for the digits read as\n"
         "an ordinary decimal."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.width())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.to_number())"
         )

// 1 6 5 0  satellite.variable.binary()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_0, VARIABLE_BINARY,
         "The bare shape, which is a name declared as a run of bits and given none.\n"
         "\n"
         "It holds nothing until something is written into it, the same state every\n"
         "other declared type starts in."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.width())"
         )

// 1 6 5 1  satellite.variable.binary.to_number
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_TO_NUMBER, VARIABLE_BINARY_TO_NUMBER,
         "What the bits are worth. `b1010.to_number()` is 10.\n"
         "\n"
         "The width does not survive the trip, because a number has no width to keep it\n"
         "in -- `b0011` and `b11` both answer 3. Ask `width()` first if you need it."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.to_number())"
         )

// 1 6 5 2  satellite.variable.binary.width
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_WIDTH, VARIABLE_BINARY_WIDTH,
         "How many bits were written. `b0011.width()` is 4.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the one question only this type can answer, and the reason a run of\n"
         "bits is not just a number: the leading zeros are part of the value, and this\n"
         "is what counts them."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b0011\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.width())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.to_number())"
         )

// 1 6 5 3  satellite.variable.binary.to_string
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_TO_STRING, VARIABLE_BINARY_TO_STRING,
         "The bits as text, exactly as `display` would print them -- the leading `b`\n"
         "included.\n"
         "\n"
         "Use it to join a run of bits onto a string, since the two types do not add to\n"
         "each other."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"the bits are \" + bits.to_string())"
         )

// 1 6 5 4  satellite.variable.binary.as_number
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_AS_NUMBER, VARIABLE_BINARY_AS_NUMBER,
         "The digits read as if they were an ordinary decimal number.\n"
         "`b1010.as_number()` is one thousand and ten.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is not the value the bits stand for -- `to_number()` is that one, and it\n"
         "answers 10. This row is for when what matters is how the run was spelled."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.as_number())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.to_number())"
         )

// 1 6 5 5  satellite.variable.binary.digits
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_DIGITS, VARIABLE_BINARY_DIGITS,
         "How many digits were written. `b1010.digits()` is 4.\n"
         "\n"
         "On a run of bits this is always the same answer as `width()`, one bit being\n"
         "one digit. It earns its own name on the other type: `x00FF.width()` is 16 and\n"
         "`x00FF.digits()` is 4, so a program handed either radix can ask how many\n"
         "characters were written without first asking which type it is holding."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.digits())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.width())"
         )

// 1 6 5 6  satellite.variable.binary.to_hex
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BINARY_TO_HEX, VARIABLE_BINARY_TO_HEX,
         "The same bits, wearing the other radix. `b1010.to_hex()` is `xA`.\n"
         "\n"
         "**It refuses when the width is not a multiple of four**, because four bits are\n"
         "one hexadecimal digit and three bits are no digit at all -- padding would write\n"
         "a bit the program never wrote. `b101.to_hex()` is refused, and `width()` is the\n"
         "question to ask first.\n"
         "\n"
         "The other direction never refuses: every hexadecimal digit has four bits, so\n"
         "`satellite.variable.hex.to_binary` always answers."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.binary bits = b1010\n"
         "satellite.console.display(bits.to_hex())"
         )

// 1 6 6  satellite.variable.bool
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_BOOL, VARIABLE_BOOL,
         "True or false, and nothing else. It is the only type a condition will\n"
         "accept, because this language has no truthiness for anything to fall back\n"
         "on.\n"
         "\n"
         "The two values it can hold are written `satellite.bool.true` and\n"
         "`satellite.bool.false`. Note the difference between this path, which is\n"
         "the type, and `satellite.bool`, which is the pair of constants."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.bool ready = satellite.bool.true\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (ready)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"ready\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 6 7  satellite.variable.date
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_DATE, VARIABLE_DATE,
         "A calendar date, apart from a moment in time.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M29 is the calendar."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 8  satellite.variable.duration
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_DURATION, VARIABLE_DURATION,
         "A length of time rather than a point in it.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M29 is the calendar."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 9  satellite.variable.expression
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_EXPRESSION, VARIABLE_EXPRESSION,
         "An expression held as a value, to be evaluated later.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built.** The path is numbered and no milestone has reached it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 10  satellite.variable.float
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FLOAT, VARIABLE_FLOAT,
         "A number with a decimal point, and the arithmetic on it is exact where a\n"
         "machine float is not: a tenth plus two tenths really is three tenths here.\n"
         "\n"
         "How many digits a division keeps is a dial you can turn, and a change to\n"
         "it is observed by the very next division rather than at the next run."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.float tenth = 0.1\n"
         "satellite.console.display(tenth + 0.2)"
         )

// 1 6 10 0  satellite.variable.float()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FLOAT_0, VARIABLE_FLOAT,
         "The bare shape. A float is a type rather than a call, so there is nothing\n"
         "to construct -- write a value with a point in it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 10 1  satellite.variable.float.to_string
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_FLOAT_TO_STRING, VARIABLE_FLOAT,
         "The value as text, exactly as `display` would print it.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the float's **only** method, and it is here because a value you can\n"
         "see on the screen and cannot put into a string is a value you cannot write\n"
         "to a file. Adding a float to a string does not work and neither does\n"
         "handing one to `write_line` -- this is how you get from the number to the\n"
         "line.\n"
         "\n"
         "Reading one back is `to_number` on the string."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.float activation = 0.4995225\n"
         "satellite.variable.string line = activation.to_string()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"wrote \" + line)"
         )

// 1 6 11  satellite.variable.hex
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX, VARIABLE_HEX,
         "A run of hexadecimal digits, written `x00FF`. **The width is part of the\n"
         "value**, so `x0009` and `x9` are not the same thing -- which is why this is a\n"
         "type of its own and not a number written in another base. It has a second\n"
         "spelling, `satellite.variable.hexadecimal`, which is the same node.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is stored as bits, four to a digit, so nothing is lost converting either\n"
         "way. Both letter cases are accepted and neither is kept: `x00ff` and `x00FF`\n"
         "are one value, and it displays in upper case.\n"
         "\n"
         "Ask `width()` for how many bits there are, `digits()` for how many digits were\n"
         "written, `to_number()` for what they are worth, and `to_binary()` for the same\n"
         "value as a run of bits.\n"
         "\n"
         "**There is no `as_number()` here**, though `satellite.variable.binary` has one.\n"
         "That row reads the characters as an ordinary decimal, and there is no decimal\n"
         "number spelled `00FF`."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.width())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.digits())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.to_number())"
         )

// 1 6 11 0  satellite.variable.hex()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX_0, VARIABLE_HEX,
         "The bare shape, which is a name declared as a run of hexadecimal digits and\n"
         "given none.\n"
         "\n"
         "It holds nothing until something is written into it, the same state every\n"
         "other declared type starts in."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.digits())"
         )

// 1 6 11 1  satellite.variable.hex.to_number
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX_TO_NUMBER, VARIABLE_HEX_TO_NUMBER,
         "What the digits are worth. `x00FF.to_number()` is 255.\n"
         "\n"
         "The width does not survive the trip, because a number has no width to keep it\n"
         "in -- `x0009` and `x9` both answer 9. Ask `width()` first if you need it."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.to_number())"
         )

// 1 6 11 2  satellite.variable.hex.width
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX_WIDTH, VARIABLE_HEX_WIDTH,
         "How many **bits** were written. `x00FF.width()` is 16, not 4.\n"
         "\n"
         "Bits and not digits, so that `width()` means one thing on both radices -- and\n"
         "so that `satellite.variable.file.write(x)`'s rule that a width must be a whole\n"
         "number of bytes reads the same here as it does on a run of bits.\n"
         "\n"
         "`digits()` is the other question, and it answers 4."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.width())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.digits())"
         )

// 1 6 11 3  satellite.variable.hex.to_string
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX_TO_STRING, VARIABLE_HEX_TO_STRING,
         "The characters `display` would print, `x` and all. `x00FF.to_string()` is\n"
         "`\"x00FF\"`.\n"
         "\n"
         "It matches the display exactly, which is what makes the printed letter case\n"
         "predictable: the case a program wrote is not stored, so this and `display` both\n"
         "answer upper.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is how a run of digits joins a string, `+` joining two strings and nothing\n"
         "else."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"colour: \" + colour.to_string())"
         )

// 1 6 11 4  satellite.variable.hex.digits
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX_DIGITS, VARIABLE_HEX_DIGITS,
         "How many digits were written. `x00FF.digits()` is 4.\n"
         "\n"
         "Always a whole number, never a remainder: a value is built from digits and each\n"
         "one is four bits, so this is `width()` divided by four exactly.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is the question `width()` does not answer, `width()` counting bits."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.digits())"
         )

// 1 6 11 5  satellite.variable.hex.to_binary
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_HEX_TO_BINARY, VARIABLE_HEX_TO_BINARY,
         "The same value, wearing the other radix. `x00FF.to_binary()` is\n"
         "`b0000000011111111`.\n"
         "\n"
         "**It never refuses**, every hexadecimal digit being exactly four bits. The\n"
         "other direction can: `satellite.variable.binary.to_hex` needs a width that is a\n"
         "multiple of four.\n"
         "\n"
         "The answer is not equal to what it came from -- `x00FF == x00FF.to_binary()` is\n"
         "false, because two different types are never equal in this language, however\n"
         "they are spelled."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.hex colour = x00FF\n"
         "satellite.console.display(colour.to_binary())"
         )

// 1 6 12  satellite.variable.network
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_NETWORK, VARIABLE_NETWORK,
         "A network connection held as a value.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M27 is the network."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 13  satellite.variable.thread
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_THREAD, VARIABLE_THREAD,
         "A line of execution running beside the others. `satellite.thread.new` is what makes one, `start()` sets it going and `join()` waits for it and hands back what the capsule returned.\n"
         "\n"
         "**A thread is a reference type.** Two names for one thread are one thread, so starting through one name and joining through the other reaches the same thread. Every other value in the language is copied when you assign it; this one is shared, the way an open file is.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Each thread gets its own variables and shares your `satellite.library`.** Locals live in a frame per call, so two threads running the same capsule never see each other's; a `satellite.library` name is the one thing they both read and write.\n"
         "\n"
         "**There is no limit on how many you may start.** If the machine will not make another, satellite tells you what the machine said."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule double_it(satellite.variable.number n) satellite.returns(satellite.variable.number)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.return(n * 2)\n"
         "}\n"
         "satellite.variable.thread t = satellite.thread.new(double_it(21))\n"
         "t.start()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(t.join())"
         )

// 1 6 13 0  satellite.variable.thread()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_THREAD_0, VARIABLE_THREAD,
         "The bare shape. A thread is written as a type, never called, so this number is reserved and nothing reaches it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 13 1  satellite.variable.thread.start()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_THREAD_START_0, VARIABLE_THREAD_START_0,
         "Sets the thread running and comes straight back -- it does not wait. The next line of your program runs while the capsule runs beside it.\n"
         "\n"
         "A thread runs once. Starting one that has already been started is refused; `satellite.thread.new` is how you get another."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule double_it(satellite.variable.number n) satellite.returns(satellite.variable.number)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.return(n * 2)\n"
         "}\n"
         "satellite.variable.thread t = satellite.thread.new(double_it(21))\n"
         "t.start()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"this printed without waiting\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(t.join())"
         )

// 1 6 13 2  satellite.variable.thread.join()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_THREAD_JOIN_0, VARIABLE_THREAD_JOIN_0,
         "Waits for the thread to finish, and answers what its capsule returned.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the only one of the three that has an answer to give, because it is the only one that waits until there is one.\n"
         "\n"
         "**If the capsule went wrong, join tells you how** -- with its own error and its own line, not a sentence about threads.\n"
         "\n"
         "You wait for a thread once. Joining one twice is refused, and so is joining one that was never started."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule double_it(satellite.variable.number n) satellite.returns(satellite.variable.number)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.return(n * 2)\n"
         "}\n"
         "satellite.variable.thread t = satellite.thread.new(double_it(21))\n"
         "t.start()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(t.join())"
         )

// 1 6 14  satellite.variable.variant
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_VARIANT, VARIABLE_VARIANT,
         "One box that can hold any type, one at a time, and can also hold nothing.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is what you want when the kind of thing is not known until the program\n"
         "runs. Ask it what it is holding before you take the value out."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant box = 5\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box)\n"
         "box = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box)"
         )

// 1 6 14 0  satellite.variable.variant()
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_VARIANT_0, VARIABLE_VARIANT,
         "The bare shape, which is a variant holding nothing.\n"
         "\n"
         "Nothing is a real state here rather than an absence, so a variant that\n"
         "has not been given a value still answers questions about itself."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant box = 5\n"
         "box.clear()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box.holds(\"nothing\"))"
         )

// 1 6 14 1  satellite.variable.variant.holding
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_VARIANT_HOLDING, VARIABLE_VARIANT_HOLDING,
         "Answers the name of the type currently in the box, as a string.\n"
         "\n"
         "A box that has been cleared answers `nothing`, which is a name like any\n"
         "other rather than a special case you have to test for separately."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant box = 5\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box.holding())\n"
         "box = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box.holding())"
         )

// 1 6 14 2  satellite.variable.variant.holds(x)
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_VARIANT_HOLDS, VARIABLE_VARIANT_HOLDS,
         "Answers true when the box is holding the type you name.\n"
         "\n"
         "It takes the type's name as a string, and `\"nothing\"` is one of the names\n"
         "you can ask about. This is the test to write before taking a value out."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant box = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box.holds(\"string\"))\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box.holds(\"nothing\"))"
         )

// 1 6 14 3  satellite.variable.variant.held
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_VARIANT_HELD, VARIABLE_VARIANT_HELD,
         "Takes the value out of the box so you can use it as its own type.\n"
         "\n"
         "Ask `holds` first. Taking out a value of a type you were not expecting is\n"
         "the mistake this pair of methods exists to prevent."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant box = \"Hello\"\n"
         "satellite.variable.string kept = box.held()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(kept.upper())"
         )

// 1 6 14 4  satellite.variable.variant.clear
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_VARIANT_CLEAR, VARIABLE_VARIANT_CLEAR,
         "Empties the box, leaving it holding nothing.\n"
         "\n"
         "Afterwards `holding()` answers `nothing` and `holds(\"nothing\")` answers\n"
         "true."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.variant box = 5\n"
         "box.clear()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(box.holding())"
         )

// 1 6 15  satellite.variable.window
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_WINDOW, VARIABLE_WINDOW,
         "A window on the screen, held as a value.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M24 is windows."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 6 16  satellite.variable.capsule
SAT_HELP(VARIABLE_CAPSULE, VARIABLE_CAPSULE,
         "A call that has not happened yet -- the capsule to run and the arguments to run it with, worked out and held.\n"
         "\n"
         "You do not declare one. `satellite.thread.new(my_capsule(x))` makes one out of the call you write inside it: `x` is worked out there and then, on the thread you are already on, and `my_capsule` is not entered until something says `start()`."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 7  satellite.random
SAT_HELP(RANDOM, RANDOM,
         "Random numbers, in four grades. `fast` is quick and good enough for a\n"
         "game, `normal` is the one to reach for by default, `ultra` is the slowest\n"
         "and the most carefully made -- and `seeded` is the odd one out.\n"
         "\n"
         "The first three throw work away before they answer, which is the only\n"
         "difference between them, and each takes the same four shapes: bare, a\n"
         "number of digits, a range, and a range with a step.\n"
         "\n"
         "`seeded` answers straight away, draws the same sequence every time from\n"
         "the same seed, and is the only one that can give you a fraction."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.normal(1, 6))"
         )

// 1 7 0  satellite.random()
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_0, RANDOM,
         "The bare shape. There is no call called `random` on its own; pick a grade\n"
         "and call that."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 7 1  satellite.random.fast()
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_FAST_0, RANDOM_FAST_0,
         "The bare shape, and it **draws nothing**. A random number with no bounds\n"
         "and no width is not a question with an answer, so calling it refuses and\n"
         "tells you the three shapes that do work: a count of digits, a low and a\n"
         "high, or a low and a high and a step.\n"
         "\n"
         "The grade itself is quick, and good enough for a game or a simulation."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.fast())"
         )

// 1 7 2  satellite.random.normal()
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_NORMAL_0, RANDOM_NORMAL_0,
         "The bare shape, and it **draws nothing**. A random number with no bounds\n"
         "and no width is not a question with an answer, so calling it refuses and\n"
         "tells you the three shapes that do work: a count of digits, a low and a\n"
         "high, or a low and a high and a step.\n"
         "\n"
         "The grade itself is the ordinary grade, and the one to use unless you have a reason not to."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.normal())"
         )

// 1 7 3  satellite.random.ultra()
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_ULTRA_0, RANDOM_ULTRA_0,
         "The bare shape, and it **draws nothing**. A random number with no bounds\n"
         "and no width is not a question with an answer, so calling it refuses and\n"
         "tells you the three shapes that do work: a count of digits, a low and a\n"
         "high, or a low and a high and a step.\n"
         "\n"
         "The grade itself is the slowest and most carefully made of the three."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.ultra())"
         )

// 1 7 4  satellite.random.fast(digits)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_FAST_DIGITS, RANDOM_FAST_0,
         "A fast random number with the many digits you ask for.\n"
         "\n"
         "Since a number here has no width, asking for a hundred digits gives you a\n"
         "hundred digits."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.fast(20))"
         )

// 1 7 5  satellite.random.fast(min, max)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_FAST_MIN_MAX, RANDOM_FAST_0,
         "A fast random number between the two bounds you give, both included."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.fast(1, 6))"
         )

// 1 7 6  satellite.random.fast(min, max, step)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_FAST_MIN_MAX_STEP, RANDOM_FAST_0,
         "A fast random number between two bounds that lands on a multiple of the\n"
         "step, so a step of 5 over 0 to 100 answers one of 0, 5, 10 and so on."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.fast(0, 100, 5))"
         )

// 1 7 7  satellite.random.normal(digits)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_NORMAL_DIGITS, RANDOM_NORMAL_0,
         "An ordinary-grade random number with as many digits as you ask for."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.normal(20))"
         )

// 1 7 8  satellite.random.normal(min, max)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_NORMAL_MIN_MAX, RANDOM_NORMAL_0,
         "An ordinary-grade random number between the two bounds you give, both\n"
         "included. This is the shape most programs want."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.normal(1, 6))"
         )

// 1 7 9  satellite.random.normal(min, max, step)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_NORMAL_MIN_MAX_STEP, RANDOM_NORMAL_0,
         "An ordinary-grade random number between two bounds, landing on a multiple\n"
         "of the step."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.normal(0, 100, 5))"
         )

// 1 7 10  satellite.random.ultra(digits)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_ULTRA_DIGITS, RANDOM_ULTRA_0,
         "A highest-grade random number with as many digits as you ask for."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.ultra(20))"
         )

// 1 7 11  satellite.random.ultra(min, max)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_ULTRA_MIN_MAX, RANDOM_ULTRA_0,
         "A highest-grade random number between the two bounds you give, both\n"
         "included."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.ultra(1, 6))"
         )

// 1 7 12  satellite.random.ultra(min, max, step)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_ULTRA_MIN_MAX_STEP, RANDOM_ULTRA_0,
         "A highest-grade random number between two bounds, landing on a multiple\n"
         "of the step."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.ultra(0, 100, 5))"
         )

// 1 7 13  satellite.random.seeded(seed)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_SEEDED_SEED, RANDOM_SEEDED_SEED,
         "Sets the seed, and answers nothing.\n"
         "\n"
         "The other three grades take their randomness from the kernel and you\n"
         "cannot ask them where they are in the sequence. This one starts where you\n"
         "tell it to, so **the same seed draws the same numbers in the same order**,\n"
         "every run. That is what you want when you are chasing something that only\n"
         "happens sometimes and need to see it twice.\n"
         "\n"
         "Write it once before the first draw. Writing it again starts over."
         ,
         "satellite.random.seeded(12345)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.seeded(1, 6, 1))"
         )

// 1 7 14  satellite.random.seeded(min, max)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_SEEDED_MIN_MAX, RANDOM_SEEDED_SEED,
         "A low and a high, inclusive at both ends -- **and this is the one draw in\n"
         "the language that can answer a fraction**.\n"
         "\n"
         "The other three grades want whole numbers, because there is no way to pick\n"
         "evenly from all the numbers between 1 and 100. This one picks evenly from\n"
         "the numbers your precision can actually hold, which is a question that does\n"
         "have an answer -- so `seeded(0, 1)` is a fraction between zero and one, and\n"
         "the bounds themselves may be fractions too.\n"
         "\n"
         "Also spelled `seeded.range(min, max)`. Seed it first or it refuses."
         ,
         "satellite.random.seeded(12345)\n"
         "satellite.variable.number fraction = satellite.random.seeded(0, 1)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(fraction)"
         )

// 1 7 15  satellite.random.seeded(min, max, step)
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_SEEDED_MIN_MAX_STEP, RANDOM_SEEDED_SEED,
         "A low, a high and a step -- every value the step lands on, and nothing\n"
         "between them.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is how you ask this grade for whole numbers: a step of 1 from 0 to 9\n"
         "is the ten integers. The step may be a fraction as well, so 0.25 from 0 to\n"
         "1 draws one of five quarters.\n"
         "\n"
         "The step has to divide the distance exactly, or the call is refused and\n"
         "tells you the last value it would have reached."
         ,
         "satellite.random.seeded(12345)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.seeded(0, 1, 0.25))"
         )

// 1 7 16  satellite.random.seeded()
SAT_HELP(RANDOM_SEEDED_0, RANDOM_SEEDED_SEED,
         "The bare shape, and it **draws nothing** -- the three other grades' bare\n"
         "shapes do not either. Supply a seed, or a low and a high, or a low and a\n"
         "high and a step."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.random.seeded())"
         )

// 1 8  satellite.file
SAT_HELP(FILE, FILE,
         "Making, opening, emptying and asking about files **by name**. The value you\n"
         "get back is a `satellite.variable.file`, and its own words -- reading, writing,\n"
         "closing -- live under that path instead of this one.\n"
         "\n"
         "The split is between what you do to a NAME and what you do through a HANDLE.\n"
         "Opening is a thing you do to a name, so it is here. Reading is a thing you do\n"
         "through a handle, so it is there. Removing is neither module's, because it\n"
         "works on files and directories alike: that is `satellite.system.delete`."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.file.exists(\"no_such_file\"))"
         )

// 1 8 0  satellite.file()
SAT_HELP(FILE_0, FILE,
         "The bare shape. `satellite.file` is a module and is never called on its own,\n"
         "so this number is reserved and nothing reaches it."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 8 1  satellite.file.new(path)
SAT_HELP(FILE_NEW_PATH, FILE_NEW_PATH,
         "Makes a file that is **not** already there, and hands back a handle already\n"
         "open on it.\n"
         "\n"
         "**It refuses to clobber.** If something is already at that path you get a\n"
         "handle that is not ok, holding the reason -- your file is untouched. That is\n"
         "deliberate: `satellite.file.open(path, \"write\")` is how you say \"empty it and\n"
         "start again\", and if `new` did that too, there would be no way left to say\n"
         "\"only if it is not there\".\n"
         "\n"
         "With no mode named it opens for reading and adding, which is the one mode\n"
         "that can both write the file you just made and read it back -- so you never\n"
         "have to open a new file a second time."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"new_demo.txt\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(f.ok())\n"
         "satellite.variable.file again = satellite.file.new(\"new_demo.txt\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(again.ok())\n"
         "satellite.console.display(again.error())\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"new_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 8 2  satellite.file.open(path, mode)
SAT_HELP(FILE_OPEN, FILE_OPEN,
         "Opens a file and hands back a handle. The second argument is a **word**\n"
         "saying what you mean to do with it:\n"
         "\n"
         "- `\"read\"` -- reading only. The file must be there.\n"
         "- `\"write\"` -- writing only, starting from empty. Makes the file if it is\n"
         "  not there, and **empties it if it is**.\n"
         "- `\"append\"` -- writing only, on the end. Makes the file if it is not there\n"
         "  and never loses what is already in it.\n"
         "- `\"read_append\"` -- both. Reads from wherever you have got to, and writes\n"
         "  on the end.\n"
         "\n"
         "There is no \"text\" or \"binary\" mode and none is needed: a satellite string\n"
         "already holds any bytes at all, and the machine does nothing to newlines.\n"
         "\n"
         "**A file that would not open is a value, not an error.** Ask the handle `ok`."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"open_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"stored\")\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.variable.file back = satellite.file.open(\"open_demo.txt\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.console.display(back.read_line())\n"
         "back.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"open_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 8 3  satellite.file.clear(path)
SAT_HELP(FILE_CLEAR, FILE_CLEAR,
         "Empties a file back to no bytes at all, **by name**, and leaves it there.\n"
         "Answers true or false.\n"
         "\n"
         "That is the difference between this and `satellite.system.delete`: after a\n"
         "clear the file still exists and anything holding it open still holds\n"
         "something; after a delete the name is gone."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"clear_demo.txt\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"something\")\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.file.clear(\"clear_demo.txt\"))\n"
         "satellite.variable.file back = satellite.file.open(\"clear_demo.txt\", \"read\")\n"
         "satellite.variable.string left = back.read_all()\n"
         "satellite.console.display(left.size())\n"
         "back.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"clear_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 8 4  satellite.file.new(path, mode)
SAT_HELP(FILE_NEW_PATH_MODE, FILE_NEW_PATH,
         "The same as `satellite.file.new(path)`, with the mode named yourself. The\n"
         "four words are the ones `satellite.file.open` takes.\n"
         "\n"
         "It still refuses to clobber: the mode says how you mean to use the file, not\n"
         "whether it may already be there."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new(\"mode_demo.txt\", \"write\")\n"
         "f.write_line(\"written\")\n"
         "f.close()\n"
         "satellite.system.delete(\"mode_demo.txt\")"
         )

// 1 8 5  satellite.file.exists(path)
SAT_HELP(FILE_EXISTS, FILE_EXISTS,
         "Whether there is a file at this path -- asked with no handle, and without\n"
         "opening anything.\n"
         "\n"
         "**A directory answers false**, which is what separates this from\n"
         "`satellite.directory.exists`. The question is \"is there a file here I could\n"
         "open\", and a directory is not one."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.file.exists(\"no_such_file\"))\n"
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.file.exists(\".\"))"
         )

// 1 9  satellite.time
SAT_HELP(TIME, TIME,
         "The clock, and waiting.\n"
         "\n"
         "Two of its words are built: asking what time it is now, and sleeping for a\n"
         "while. Holding a moment in a variable is a later milestone."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.time.now())"
         )

// 1 9 0  satellite.time()
SAT_HELP(TIME_0, TIME,
         "The bare shape. The clock is a module rather than something you\n"
         "construct; one of the words underneath is what you call."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 9 1  satellite.time.now
SAT_HELP(TIME_NOW, TIME_NOW,
         "What time it is, read at the moment you ask.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is one of the two sources of nondeterminism in the language, the dice\n"
         "being the other, which is why they were built together and why a program\n"
         "that uses neither runs the same way every time."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.time.now())"
         )

// 1 9 2  satellite.time.new
SAT_HELP(TIME_NEW, TIME_NEW,
         "Makes a moment of your own rather than reading the clock.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M29 is the calendar milestone that decides what a time\n"
         "value can be."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 9 3  satellite.time.sleep(n)
SAT_HELP(TIME_SLEEP, TIME_SLEEP,
         "Stops the program for the number of milliseconds you give, then carries\n"
         "on.\n"
         "\n"
         "Ctrl-C still reaches a program that is sleeping, so a long wait is not a\n"
         "program you have to kill."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(\"before\")\n"
         "satellite.time.sleep(10)\n"
         "satellite.console.display(\"after\")"
         )

// 1 10  satellite.spacesuit
SAT_HELP(SPACESUIT, SPACESUIT,
         "A spacesuit: a body of code kept apart, with its own names, that a\n"
         "program brings in rather than writes out.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M26. Its neighbours `satellite.protected` and\n"
         "`satellite.public` are the words that say what a spacesuit shows and what\n"
         "it keeps to itself."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 10 0  satellite.spacesuit()
SAT_HELP(SPACESUIT_0, SPACESUIT,
         "The bare shape. **Not built** — M26."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 11  satellite.protected
SAT_HELP(PROTECTED, PROTECTED,
         "Marks something as belonging to the spacesuit that declares it, and not\n"
         "visible outside.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M26."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 11 0  satellite.protected()
SAT_HELP(PROTECTED_0, PROTECTED,
         "The bare shape. **Not built** — M26."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 12  satellite.public
SAT_HELP(PUBLIC, PUBLIC,
         "Marks something as visible to whatever brings the spacesuit in.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Not built** — M26."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 12 0  satellite.public()
SAT_HELP(PUBLIC_0, PUBLIC,
         "The bare shape. **Not built** — M26."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 13  satellite.statement
SAT_HELP(STATEMENT, STATEMENT,
         "The statements that change what runs next, rather than what a value is.\n"
         "There are exactly four of them and every one takes a block in braces on\n"
         "the lines below it.\n"
         "\n"
         "The brace goes on its own line, under the head, which is how every file in\n"
         "this language is written."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.bool ready = satellite.bool.true\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (ready)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"go\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 13 0  satellite.statement()
SAT_HELP(STATEMENT_0, STATEMENT,
         "The bare shape. There is no statement called `statement`; the word is the\n"
         "family and one of the four underneath is what you write.\n"
         "\n"
         "Asking help about the family is how you see all four at once."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 13 1  satellite.statement.if
SAT_HELP(STATEMENT_IF, STATEMENT_IF,
         "Runs its block when the condition is true. The condition has to be a\n"
         "`satellite.variable.bool` and nothing else.\n"
         "\n"
         "**There is no truthiness here.** A number that happens to be zero is not\n"
         "false, an empty string is not false, and a value that is holding nothing\n"
         "is not false. Compare something, or hold a bool, and the test is honest."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number count = 3\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (count > 0)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"there is at least one\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 13 2  satellite.statement.for
SAT_HELP(STATEMENT_FOR, STATEMENT_FOR,
         "Counts. The head holds three parts separated by semicolons: what to\n"
         "declare before the first pass, the test that decides whether to go round\n"
         "again, and what to do at the end of each pass.\n"
         "\n"
         "The variable declared in the head belongs to the loop and is gone after\n"
         "it."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number total = 0\n"
         "satellite.statement.for (satellite.variable.number i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    total = total + i\n"
         "}\n"
         "satellite.console.display(total)"
         )

// 1 13 3  satellite.statement.while
SAT_HELP(STATEMENT_WHILE, STATEMENT_WHILE,
         "Goes round for as long as the condition holds, testing before each pass\n"
         "rather than after, so a condition that is false to begin with runs the\n"
         "block no times at all.\n"
         "\n"
         "Something inside the block has to change what the condition reads, or the\n"
         "loop does not end. Ctrl-C stops one that does not."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.number countdown = 3\n"
         "satellite.statement.while (countdown > 0)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(countdown)\n"
         "    countdown = countdown - 1\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 13 4  satellite.statement.else
SAT_HELP(STATEMENT_ELSE, STATEMENT_ELSE,
         "The other way. It follows an `if` block and runs when that condition was\n"
         "false, and it takes no condition of its own.\n"
         "\n"
         "It is written as its own statement on the line after the if block's\n"
         "closing brace."
         ,
         "satellite.variable.bool loud = satellite.bool.false\n"
         "satellite.statement.if (loud)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"loud is true\")\n"
         "}\n"
         "satellite.statement.else\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(\"loud is false\")\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14  satellite.library
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY, LIBRARY,
         "Where the program's own globals live, and where the machine's settings\n"
         "are read and written.\n"
         "\n"
         "A name you make under `satellite.library` is a global: it is declared at\n"
         "the top of a file, outside any capsule, and every capsule can see it."
         ,
         "satellite.console.display(satellite.library.system.float_digits)"
         )

// 1 14 0  satellite.library()
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_0, LIBRARY,
         "The bare shape. The library is a place rather than a thing to call, and\n"
         "one of the words underneath is what you write."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 14 1  satellite.library.main
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN, LIBRARY_MAIN,
         "Where the program's own starting point lives, and the one thing under it:\n"
         "the arguments object.\n"
         "\n"
         "You do not write this path. `satellite.main`'s parameter is how the object\n"
         "is reached, and the name is whatever you called that parameter."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 14 1 0  satellite.library.main()
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_0, LIBRARY_MAIN,
         "The bare shape. `main` is a place rather than a thing to call — the capsule\n"
         "you declare is `satellite.capsule satellite.main(...)`, and this path is\n"
         "where the numbering puts what that capsule is handed."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 14 1 1  satellite.library.main.arguments
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS,
         "The arguments object: the command line, and the facts about the machine\n"
         "and the person running the program.\n"
         "\n"
         "**The name is yours.** It is reached through whatever you called the\n"
         "parameter on `satellite.main`, and seven spellings are accepted for it —\n"
         "`arguments`, `argument`, `argumentz`, `args`, `argz`, `arg` and `argv`.\n"
         "\n"
         "Displaying it bare prints all of it, one fact per line. The words that were\n"
         "typed are reached by number and the machine's answers by name."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 0  satellite.library.main.arguments()
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_0, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS,
         "The bare shape. The object is handed to the program rather than made by it,\n"
         "so there is nothing to call: write the parameter's name."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE,
         "What the machine is — written on its own it displays all seven of its\n"
         "facts at once, as a map.\n"
         "\n"
         "Every group under `arguments` answers this way, so you can print a whole\n"
         "section without naming each word in it."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 0  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine()
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_0, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE,
         "The bare shape. Writing `machine` with or without the parentheses reaches\n"
         "the same answer, which is all of its children together."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 1  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.cores
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_CORES, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_CORES,
         "How many physical cores the machine has, as a number.\n"
         "\n"
         "Counted from the kernel's own topology and never as threads divided by two,\n"
         "which is wrong on any machine that does not run two threads per core."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.cores)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 2  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.cpu
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_CPU, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_CPU,
         "What the processor calls itself — the model name, as a string.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the marketing name a person recognises. `architecture` is the\n"
         "instruction set, which is a different fact and has its own word."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.cpu)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 3  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.threads
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_THREADS, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_THREADS,
         "How many threads the machine can run at once, as a number, which is usually\n"
         "more than the number of cores.\n"
         "\n"
         "This is the same number `\\threads` puts in a string and the same number the\n"
         "configuration file writes as `THREAD_COUNT`. Three ways to ask, one place\n"
         "that knows."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.threads)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 4  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.architecture
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_ARCHITECTURE, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_ARCHITECTURE,
         "What kind of processor this is — `x86_64` on this machine. This is the\n"
         "machine's instruction set and not its model name; `cpu` is the model."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.architecture)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 5  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.byte_order
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_BYTE_ORDER, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_BYTE_ORDER,
         "Which end of a number this machine stores first — `little` or `big`.\n"
         "\n"
         "Answered by looking at a value rather than by trusting a compiler macro, so\n"
         "it is right on a machine the macro does not cover."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.byte_order)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 6  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.page_size
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_PAGE_SIZE, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_PAGE_SIZE,
         "How many bytes are in a page of memory, as a number. 4096 on most machines,\n"
         "and worth asking rather than assuming on the ones where it is not."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.page_size)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 1 7  satellite.library.main.arguments.machine.pointer_bits
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_POINTER_BITS, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MACHINE_POINTER_BITS,
         "How wide an address is on this machine — 64 on anything current, as a\n"
         "number. It describes the running interpreter, so a 32-bit build on a 64-bit\n"
         "machine answers 32."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.machine.pointer_bits)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 2  satellite.library.main.arguments.memory
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MEMORY, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MEMORY,
         "How much memory the machine has — written on its own it displays its\n"
         "children, which today is `total`.\n"
         "\n"
         "**Free memory is not here.** `satellite.system.memory.free()` is what\n"
         "answers that, in whichever unit you ask for; this section is what the\n"
         "program was told about the machine when it started."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.memory)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 2 0  satellite.library.main.arguments.memory()
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MEMORY_0, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MEMORY,
         "The bare shape, which is the same answer as the word on its own: all of\n"
         "this section's children together."
         ,
         ""
         )

// 1 14 1 1 2 1  satellite.library.main.arguments.memory.total
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MEMORY_TOTAL, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_MEMORY_TOTAL,
         "How much memory the machine has in total, in whole megabytes, as a number.\n"
         "\n"
         "The same number `\\memtotal` puts in a string. `satellite.system.memory\n"
         ".total(\"mb\")` answers the exact figure with its fraction, and this is that\n"
         "figure with the fraction dropped — the two agree at whole megabytes, which\n"
         "is the resolution this one has."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.memory.total)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 3  satellite.library.main.arguments.username
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_USERNAME, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_USERNAME,
         "Who is running the program, as a string.\n"
         "\n"
         "Taken from the password database rather than from `$USER`, because the\n"
         "environment is something a shell was told and the kernel's answer is what\n"
         "the process actually is."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.username)\n"
         "}"
         )

// 1 14 1 1 4  satellite.library.main.arguments.system
SAT_HELP(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_SYSTEM, LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_SYSTEM,
         "What the operating system is: its name, its kernel, which distribution it\n"
         "is, and what this machine is called. Written on its own it displays all\n"
         "seven at once.\n"
         "\n"
         "One fact per word rather than one sentence to read, so a program that wants\n"
         "the kernel does not have to take a string apart."
         ,
         "satellite.capsule satellite.main(satellite.container.list<satellite.variable.string> arguments)\n"
         "{\n"
         "    satellite.console.display(arguments.system)\n"
         "}"
         )

